  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 208 AM CDT Thu Sep 17 2026\n\nWhile the evening sounding only showed PWAT values of 1.72\", \nsatellite is showing PWAT values of 1.9-2.1\" moving its way into \nSouth Texas. This along with an mid-level disturbance will aid in \nthe development of showers and thunderstorms. Rain chances will \nslowly increase tonight peaking tomorrow between 35-70% along the \neastern half and 20-40% across the western half of South Texas. Rain \nchances will decrease this weekend to under 15% rebounding back to \nthe 20-40% range early to mid next week each afternoon with the \nseabreeze. While warm temperatures will remain warm, heat index \nvalues are expected to drop with the increased cloud cover lowering \nout heat risk for the next few days. While there is still plenty of \nuncertainty, coastal flooding could still come into play as models \ncontinue to show a long easterly fetch developing across the \nnorthern gulf waters this week which may help push water farther up \nthe beaches. Will continue to monitor trends for slightly higher \nthan normal tides and/or an increased rip current risk.  At this \ntime, minor coastal flooding is not anticipated but will continue to \nbe monitored.",
